1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,119
Welcome to Golden Hour — Hollywood, Then and Now.

2
00:00:04,119 --> 00:00:06,598
Some faces never really leave us.

3
00:00:06,598 --> 00:00:14,449
You hear one song, one laugh, one famous line, and a whole afternoon of your childhood comes rushing back.

4
00:00:14,449 --> 00:00:24,778
Tonight: thirty-five of the brightest stars of the sixties, seventies and eighties — and every single one of them is still with us in 2026.

5
00:00:24,778 --> 00:00:28,497
So keep count of how many you still remember.

6
00:00:28,497 --> 00:00:36,761
And stay to the very end — because the final name on our list owns a record twenty-one Oscar nominations.

7
00:00:36,761 --> 00:00:39,240
The category, as always, is Meryl.

8
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:49,802
Act One: Those We Never Forgot — the movie stars who owned the big screen.

9
00:00:49,802 --> 00:00:59,180
And we begin with a man who, in December of 2025, did something only a handful of performers in Hollywood history have ever done.

10
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:10,491
Put him in a chimney-sweep's cap and a cockney grin, and Mary Poppins plays in your head all over again.

11
00:01:10,491 --> 00:01:18,798
But Bert was only the beginning — five decades of television followed, from The Dick Van Dyke Show to Diagnosis: Murder.

12
00:01:18,798 --> 00:01:23,544
At 98, he became the oldest performer ever to win an Emmy.

13
00:01:23,544 --> 00:01:31,060
And in December 2025, Dick Van Dyke blew out one hundred candles — still dancing, still grinning, still ours.

14
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:40,777
You may remember the woman holding Cary Grant's hand on the train in North by Northwest.

15
00:01:40,777 --> 00:01:45,064
But the Oscar came earlier — On the Waterfront, 1954, opposite Brando.

16
00:01:45,064 --> 00:01:49,709
She never played the starlet; she played the woman who knew too much.

17
00:01:49,709 --> 00:01:58,642
Born on the Fourth of July, 1924, Eva Marie Saint is one hundred and two years old — the oldest living Oscar winner on Earth.

18
00:01:58,642 --> 00:02:01,500
The Academy's living legend, still writing her story.

19
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:14,456
A cigar, a squint, three dollar bills — the Man with No Name didn't need dialogue to own the screen.

20
00:02:14,456 --> 00:02:23,859
Dirty Harry made him an icon; the director's chair made him immortal — four Oscars, from Unforgiven to Million Dollar Baby.

21
00:02:23,859 --> 00:02:26,546
Clint Eastwood turned 96 in May.

22
00:02:26,546 --> 00:02:30,576
His son says the Old Man has finally retired.

23
00:02:30,576 --> 00:02:37,740
After seventy years in front of and behind the camera — he's earned every silent sunset.

24
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:48,129
One look from her, and you understood why the whole world fell for Italian cinema.

25
00:02:48,129 --> 00:03:00,906
She was a war child from the slums of Naples — and in 1961, Two Women made her the first actress ever to win an Oscar for a foreign-language performance.

26
00:03:00,906 --> 00:03:11,980
At 91, Sophia Loren remains the last great lady of Europe's golden screen — crowned in 2020 by one final, unforgettable performance in The Life Ahead.

27
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:23,243
Just one octave of Supercalifragilistic, or one hilltop of Salzburg, and you can hear her instantly.

28
00:03:23,243 --> 00:03:29,143
Then, in 1997, the voice that defined two generations was silenced by surgery.

29
00:03:29,143 --> 00:03:39,130
Hollywood braced for tragedy — instead, she built a second act: The Princess Diaries, and the voice of Lady Whistledown narrating Bridgerton.

30
00:03:39,130 --> 00:03:47,300
Dame Julie Andrews turned 90 last October — proof that even when the singing stops, the music doesn't.

31
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:57,979
In 1961, Anita danced through West Side Story like a flame — and took home the Oscar.

32
00:03:57,979 --> 00:04:10,158
Sixty years later, Steven Spielberg handed her a new role in the very same story — making her the only performer to appear in both versions of a Best Picture winner.

33
00:04:10,158 --> 00:04:19,980
Ninety-four years old, an EGOT before the term even existed, Rita Moreno is still onstage — because standing still was never part of her choreography.

34
00:04:23,980 --> 00:04:33,652
Barbarella in space, Bree Daniels on the phone — in the seventies, Jane Fonda was the most fearless woman on screen.

35
00:04:33,652 --> 00:04:45,166
Two Oscars later — Klute, Coming Home — she traded the screen for protest lines, and paid for it: the lists, the arrests, the nickname.

36
00:04:45,166 --> 00:04:52,075
At 88, she is still marching — climate protests, bare-knuckle activism, Grace and Frankie reruns.

37
00:04:52,075 --> 00:04:54,378
Hollywood trained her to perform.

38
00:04:54,378 --> 00:04:56,220
She chose to fight.

39
00:05:00,220 --> 00:05:03,617
That grey suit at the foot of the stairs.

40
00:05:03,617 --> 00:05:10,788
Vertigo made Kim Novak the most mysterious blonde of the fifties — Madeleine and Judy, the same woman twice.

41
00:05:10,788 --> 00:05:20,602
But Hollywood was a world she chose to leave: painting, poetry, horses, and a quiet life in Oregon, far from the spotlight that nearly broke her.

42
00:05:20,602 --> 00:05:25,131
At 93, Venice honored her with a Golden Lion for lifetime achievement.

43
00:05:25,131 --> 00:05:29,660
She came back on her own terms — and then went home.

44
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:38,280
Act Two: The Faces in Our Living Room.

45
00:05:38,280 --> 00:05:41,600
Movies were events — but television was family.

46
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:49,484
These faces sat across our dinner tables for twenty years, and every one of them is still with us.

47
00:05:52,884 --> 00:05:55,296
Space — the final frontier.

48
00:05:55,296 --> 00:05:59,638
You cannot read that line in any other voice.

49
00:05:59,638 --> 00:06:03,979
Typecast forever as Kirk, he bent the typecast: T.

50
00:06:03,979 --> 00:06:04,461
J.

51
00:06:04,461 --> 00:06:08,803
Hooker, Denny Crane, the Priceline pitchman with a wink.

52
00:06:08,803 --> 00:06:15,074
At ninety, William Shatner flew to actual space — and came home changed.

53
00:06:15,074 --> 00:06:19,898
Now ninety-five, he still tours, still records, still boldly goes.

54
00:06:23,898 --> 00:06:30,736
One tug of that curtain-rod earring, and The Carol Burnett Show made America laugh before dinner.

55
00:06:30,736 --> 00:06:38,000
Eleven seasons, a Tarzan yell that still stops traffic, and a generosity that built a comedy dynasty.

56
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:46,120
At ninety-three, Carol Burnett remains the first lady of television comedy — and still never takes herself too seriously.

57
00:06:50,119 --> 00:06:53,102
Feathers, the card-playing beauty of Rio Bravo.

58
00:06:53,102 --> 00:07:02,053
Twenty years later she flipped the script: Police Woman made Pepper Anderson the first woman to headline a hit cop show.

59
00:07:02,053 --> 00:07:10,577
This very month, Angie Dickinson turns ninety-five — rarely photographed, famously picky, and still the coolest face of the seventies.

60
00:07:14,577 --> 00:07:21,864
M*A*S*H gave television its greatest joke-teller and its deepest wound, and Alan Alda carried both for eleven seasons.

61
00:07:21,864 --> 00:07:29,151
Then he built a second life teaching empathy — to doctors, to engineers, one conversation at a time.

62
00:07:29,151 --> 00:07:34,009
At ninety, he still hosts, still writes, still asks the better question.

63
00:07:38,009 --> 00:07:38,743
The blink.

64
00:07:38,743 --> 00:07:39,477
The ponytail.

65
00:07:39,477 --> 00:07:40,945
The pink harem pants.

66
00:07:40,945 --> 00:07:45,349
Five seasons of I Dream of Jeannie made her immortal in syndication.

67
00:07:45,349 --> 00:07:54,524
At ninety-five, Barbara Eden still greets convention crowds with the arms-crossed nod — and fans still ask for the wish she invented sixty years ago.

68
00:07:54,524 --> 00:07:55,992
She always grants it.

69
00:07:59,992 --> 00:08:03,935
Ginger Grant — the movie star stranded on a three-hour tour.

70
00:08:03,935 --> 00:08:10,388
When the island went off the air, Tina Louise walked away from Ginger forever: no reunions, no sequels.

71
00:08:10,388 --> 00:08:15,765
At ninety-two, she is the last islander still with us, having outlived every single castmate.

72
00:08:15,765 --> 00:08:17,199
She made it home.

73
00:08:21,199 --> 00:08:28,596
For a decade, Florence kept the Jeffersons' penthouse honest, one withering look at George at a time.

74
00:08:28,596 --> 00:08:32,947
Then she produced 227 into a hit nobody believed in.

75
00:08:32,947 --> 00:08:39,039
At ninety-five, Marla Gibbs is still booking roles — steady work across eight decades.

76
00:08:39,039 --> 00:08:40,344
Moving on up.

77
00:08:44,344 --> 00:08:48,301
He replaced a legend on television's toughest stage — B.

78
00:08:48,301 --> 00:08:48,697
J.

79
00:08:48,697 --> 00:08:53,050
Hunnicutt stepping into the 4077th and making us stop missing Trapper.

80
00:08:53,050 --> 00:08:57,799
Off camera he became television's conscience: prison reform, human rights, refugee missions.

81
00:08:57,799 --> 00:09:04,526
Fifty years on — with Shelley by his side — Mike Farrell simply will not sit down.

82
00:09:08,526 --> 00:09:15,353
Kelly Garrett — the calm center of Charlie's Angels, the only Angel who answered every single call.

83
00:09:15,353 --> 00:09:20,975
Then she built a fashion empire from a Kmart rack, worth hundreds of millions.

84
00:09:20,975 --> 00:09:26,999
At eighty, Jaclyn Smith still posts the elegance that made the seventies stand up straight.

85
00:09:32,899 --> 00:09:33,699
Quick pause.

86
00:09:33,699 --> 00:09:35,699
That's seventeen faces so far.

87
00:09:35,699 --> 00:09:37,699
How many did you remember?

88
00:09:37,699 --> 00:09:44,099
Drop your number in the comments — then stay right there, because the voices are next.

89
00:09:44,099 --> 00:09:45,699
Act Three begins now.

90
00:09:51,099 --> 00:09:54,882
Act Three: The Soundtrack of Our Lives.

91
00:09:54,882 --> 00:10:02,988
Before playlists, there were radios — and these are the voices that lived inside them.

92
00:10:02,988 --> 00:10:06,230
Every one of them still breathing.

93
00:10:09,630 --> 00:10:15,586
One beat-up guitar named Trigger, one bandana, a voice like a Texas gravel road.

94
00:10:15,586 --> 00:10:23,244
Nashville shut him out, so he invented outlaw country — then built Farm Aid into a forty-year promise.

95
00:10:23,244 --> 00:10:26,222
At ninety-three, Willie Nelson is still recording.

96
00:10:26,222 --> 00:10:28,349
The road goes on forever.

97
00:10:28,349 --> 00:10:29,625
So does he.

98
00:10:33,625 --> 00:10:39,357
Chances are — two syllables, and forty years of school dances come flooding back.

99
00:10:39,357 --> 00:10:45,498
In 2025, after nearly seventy years on the road, Johnny Mathis said goodbye to touring.

100
00:10:45,498 --> 00:10:47,545
This month he turns ninety-one.

101
00:10:47,545 --> 00:10:50,411
The voice stepped back; the man didn't.

102
00:10:50,411 --> 00:10:52,458
Chances are, he's still humming.

103
00:10:56,458 --> 00:11:03,058
The hips, the sweat, It's Not Unusual — and a rain of lingerie over Las Vegas.

104
00:11:03,058 --> 00:11:07,596
The sex symbol outlived the seventies by simply being a singer.

105
00:11:07,596 --> 00:11:16,259
At eighty-six, Sir Tom Jones still tours, still coaches on The Voice, and still dares you to sit down during Delilah.

106
00:11:20,259 --> 00:11:26,674
That falsetto — Sherry, Big Girls Don't Cry, Can't Take My Eyes Off You.

107
00:11:26,674 --> 00:11:31,256
Jersey street kids who outsold the British Invasion at home.

108
00:11:31,256 --> 00:11:36,297
At eighty-nine, Frankie Valli became a father again — to twins.

109
00:11:36,297 --> 00:11:39,046
Now ninety-two, he is still onstage.

110
00:11:39,046 --> 00:11:40,879
Oh, what a night.

111
00:11:40,879 --> 00:11:42,712
He keeps having them.

112
00:11:46,712 --> 00:11:53,220
Four notes of Walk On By, and Burt Bacharach's whole songbook walks through the door.

113
00:11:53,220 --> 00:11:58,426
In her eighties she became the warmest, funniest woman on social media.

114
00:11:58,426 --> 00:12:04,500
Dionne Warwick is eighty-five — still touring, still reading your comments, still that voice.

115
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:15,886
Sweet Caroline — three chords, one ba-ba-ba, and every stadium on Earth becomes a choir.

116
00:12:15,886 --> 00:12:19,332
Parkinson's ended the touring, never the songs.

117
00:12:19,332 --> 00:12:27,703
Broadway built A Beautiful Noise around him, and on opening night he stood up and sang along.

118
00:12:27,703 --> 00:12:30,657
At eighty-five, the melody never shakes.

119
00:12:34,657 --> 00:12:42,373
At sixteen he wrote Diana, and it sold nine million copies before he could legally drive.

120
00:12:42,373 --> 00:12:49,124
Then came My Way for Sinatra, the Tonight Show theme, hits for Michael Jackson.

121
00:12:49,124 --> 00:12:56,358
At eighty-five, Paul Anka still plays Las Vegas — the last of the great songmen.

122
00:13:00,358 --> 00:13:00,835
GOLD-finger.

123
00:13:00,835 --> 00:13:07,507
One word — held, belted, immortal — and James Bond found his golden voice.

124
00:13:07,507 --> 00:13:16,563
Goldfinger, Diamonds Are Forever, Moonraker: three themes across three decades, and no other singer has ever carried even two.

125
00:13:16,563 --> 00:13:18,946
Dame Shirley Bassey is eighty-nine.

126
00:13:18,946 --> 00:13:20,852
The diamonds are forever.

127
00:13:20,852 --> 00:13:22,282
So is she.

128
00:13:26,282 --> 00:13:32,114
That warm brass fanfare — the Tijuana Brass outselling the Beatles in 1966.

129
00:13:32,114 --> 00:13:43,777
The label he built became home to the Carpenters and the Police — and then he gave his fortune away, to teach the next generation music.

130
00:13:43,777 --> 00:13:47,815
At ninety-one, Herb Alpert still paints, sculpts and records.

131
00:13:47,815 --> 00:13:49,609
Rise never really ends.

132
00:13:53,609 --> 00:14:00,432
Go-go boots on a candy-colored soundstage — the sassiest warning shot of 1966.

133
00:14:00,432 --> 00:14:10,929
A year later she recorded Somethin' Stupid with her father, still the only father-daughter duet ever to reach number one.

134
00:14:10,929 --> 00:14:16,177
Nancy Sinatra is eighty-six — still walking it her way.

135
00:14:22,077 --> 00:14:24,524
That's twenty-seven, and you're doing great.

136
00:14:24,524 --> 00:14:29,419
If you've recognized every single one, type STILL SHARP in the comments.

137
00:14:29,419 --> 00:14:35,130
One act left — and it belongs to the biggest names in the business.

138
00:14:40,530 --> 00:14:43,453
Act Four: Still Turning Heads.

139
00:14:43,453 --> 00:14:45,207
Some stars faded.

140
00:14:45,207 --> 00:14:46,376
These didn't.

141
00:14:46,376 --> 00:14:51,637
Eight living proofs that the seventies never really ended.

142
00:14:55,037 --> 00:14:56,006
Here's Johnny!

143
00:14:56,006 --> 00:15:02,306
— an axe through a door, and the most famous grin in movies.

144
00:15:02,306 --> 00:15:09,574
Three Oscars, twelve nominations — then, around 2010, the greatest recluse in Hollywood simply stopped.

145
00:15:09,574 --> 00:15:11,028
No farewell tour.

146
00:15:11,028 --> 00:15:11,997
No explanation.

147
00:15:11,997 --> 00:15:19,266
At eighty-nine, Jack Nicholson surfaces at Lakers games — gray beard, sunglasses, that same grin.

148
00:15:23,266 --> 00:15:30,885
A smuggler's smirk in a cantina — Han Solo was never the hero; he was something better: a survivor.

149
00:15:30,885 --> 00:15:36,500
A carpenter turned superstar, a legend who walked away from two real plane crashes.

150
00:15:36,500 --> 00:15:40,911
At eighty-four, Harrison Ford is still first on the call sheet.

151
00:15:40,911 --> 00:15:42,114
The fedora fits.

152
00:15:42,114 --> 00:15:43,317
It always will.

153
00:15:47,317 --> 00:15:51,345
Half of Sonny and Cher, in bell-bottoms and deadpan.

154
00:15:51,345 --> 00:16:00,295
Then the reinventions: an Oscar for Moonstruck, the Believe vocal that rewired pop, a farewell tour now twenty years long.

155
00:16:00,295 --> 00:16:07,903
Cher turned eighty this May — the only artist with a number-one single in seven consecutive decades.

156
00:16:07,903 --> 00:16:09,246
Goddesses don't retire.

157
00:16:13,246 --> 00:16:19,776
That giggle on Laugh-In — the dumb blonde who was always the smartest person in the room.

158
00:16:19,776 --> 00:16:27,457
An Oscar at twenty-four, thirty years of pure star power, and forty years with Kurt Russell without a single wedding.

159
00:16:27,457 --> 00:16:33,987
At eighty, Goldie Hawn laughs exactly like 1968 — and refuses, on principle, to act her age.

160
00:16:37,987 --> 00:16:45,235
The Divine Miss M — bathhouse sass, Wind Beneath My Wings, every tear-jerker your mother played twice.

161
00:16:45,235 --> 00:16:51,629
Then she took stardom to the streets, replanting New York's community gardens block by block.

162
00:16:51,629 --> 00:16:55,893
At eighty, Bette Midler still sings like a weather system.

163
00:16:55,893 --> 00:16:57,172
Some stars twinkle.

164
00:16:57,172 --> 00:16:58,877
The Divine One illuminates.

165
00:17:02,877 --> 00:17:08,689
Ernestine, the telephone operator — one ringy-dingy — the funniest switchboard in America.

166
00:17:08,689 --> 00:17:14,500
Nine to Five, then Grace and Frankie made her a legend twice over.

167
00:17:14,500 --> 00:17:21,206
At eighty-seven, Lily Tomlin still tours new material with her wife and partner Jane Wagner.

168
00:17:21,206 --> 00:17:24,335
She sounds like she's just getting started.

169
00:17:28,335 --> 00:17:30,925
Say hello to my little friend.

170
00:17:30,925 --> 00:17:35,242
You heard it in his voice before the sentence ended.

171
00:17:35,242 --> 00:17:39,991
The Godfather, Scarface, an Oscar finally with Scent of a Woman.

172
00:17:39,991 --> 00:17:47,330
At eighty-six, Al Pacino is a father again — his youngest son born when he was eighty-three.

173
00:17:47,330 --> 00:17:49,920
Michael Corleone left the family business.

174
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:51,647
Al never wanted out.

175
00:17:55,647 --> 00:18:01,029
Sophie's Choice — one scene, and cinema redefined what acting could cost.

176
00:18:01,029 --> 00:18:09,103
A record twenty-one nominations, three Oscars — from Miranda Priestly's withering stare to Only Murders in the Building.

177
00:18:09,103 --> 00:18:14,934
At seventy-seven, Meryl Streep stole a comedy after conquering five decades of drama.

178
00:18:14,934 --> 00:18:16,728
The category is acting.

179
00:18:16,728 --> 00:18:18,522
It was always Meryl.

180
00:18:20,922 --> 00:18:22,154
And that's thirty-five.

181
00:18:22,154 --> 00:18:24,209
Thirty-five faces time didn't erase.

182
00:18:24,209 --> 00:18:33,657
Before we go — this past year, Golden Hour said goodbye to Dolly Parton, Robert Duvall, Sam Neill, Chuck Norris and Tim Curry.

183
00:18:33,657 --> 00:18:36,122
The screen gets quieter every year.

184
00:18:36,122 --> 00:18:41,874
But tonight proved something too: thirty-five legends, still here, still shining at golden hour.

185
00:18:41,874 --> 00:18:44,749
So — how many did you remember?

186
00:18:44,749 --> 00:18:53,376
Leave your number in the comments, and subscribe, because part two is already loading: the dames and knights across the pond.

187
00:18:53,376 --> 00:18:55,430
Golden hour never really ends.

188
00:18:55,430 --> 00:18:58,717
It just waits for us to look up.

